  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "For positive numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) the operation \\(\\spadesuit (x,y)\\) is defined as \\[\\spadesuit (x,y) = x-\\dfrac{1}{y}\\] What is \\(\\spadesuit (2,\\spadesuit (2,2))?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": "4/3"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2010-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Evaluating the inner expression, we get \\[ \\spadesuit (2, 2) = 2 - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{2}. \\] Then we have \\[ \\spadesuit \\left(2, \\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = 2 - \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\dfrac{4}{3}. \\]\n\nThus, C is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Evaluate the inner \\(\\spadesuit\\) expression first",
        "\\(\\spadesuit(2,2)=2-\\dfrac12\\), then use that as the second input"
      ],
